WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.020
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:09.300
This is Ben Busby and I'm the genomics outreach
coordinator and bioinformatics training lead

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.650
for NCBI.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:18.250
I am joined by Peter Cooper who is the strategic
communications coordinator for NCBI.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:23.390
We will be talking about downloading human
genome sequence, variants and annotation from

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.900
NCBI.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.680
Today I will do most of this as a live demonstration
but I prepared a set of slides covering most

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.610
of what I talk about as a backup.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:41.200
The slides are available at this link, go.usa.gov/x58jN,
as well as slideshare.net/benbusby.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:47.670
On both of those resources there are other
slides and presentations so feel free to check

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:52.910
them out or you can always Google for NCBI
webinars.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:56.910
If most of you are like me, you probably start
your search with Google.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:03.120
This is what we find most users do and one
thing I might Google for is NCBI human genome

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.120
resources.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:11.140
I can open up an Incognito tab just so I don't
get autocomplete.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:17.420
What I might Google for is NCBI human genome
resources.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:25.360
I don't know why my auto completes are still
showing up, but that is not an issue for today.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.560
The first link I see is human genome resources
at NCBI.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.049
We modified this page about one year ago.

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:39.220
We put a whole bunch of new bells and whistles
in, and what we have done recently is moved

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.740
up the download section of the page and made
it easier to use.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:48.009
One thing you could do on this page is search
for human genes and it will send you to the

00:01:48.009 --> 00:01:56.890
NCBI Gene page and you can click on the ideogram
to access the genome data viewer in that region.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:06.240
What I want to talk about today is downloading
various sequences as well as other NCBI resources

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.420
through this simple to use table.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:17.360
If you want the latest reference genome sequence
in either GRCh38, the new version of the human

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:24.209
genome, about 30% of you are using that at
this point and 70% are still using the old

00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:25.680
one, GRCh37.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:33.819
If you are interested in the differences between
GRCh38 and37, I would encourage you to check

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:37.340
out the genome reference consortium webpage.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:43.239
You can do that by googling genome reference
consortium and you can check that out and

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:49.110
it will tell you all kinds of things about
GRCh37 and GRCh38.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:57.020
Anyway, nn this page this gives you quick,
direct links to download the reference genome

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:02.460
sequence, so this is going to be the FASTA
file, gzipped, but that should be accessible

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.909
to most of you, as well as the gff3 file.

00:03:06.909 --> 00:03:12.730
Here you can get all of the annotations that
NCBI has for that file.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:16.210
Some of you may just want RefSeq transcripts.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:22.670
These are just the mRNA sequences that are
transcribed from the GRC38 human genome, the

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:26.390
ones that we are fairly certain are transcribed.

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:30.370
You can get all protein sequences; this is
a protein FASTA.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:36.840
And you can also get a vcf for ClinVar.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.710
This is a quick way to get it, as well as
the vcf for dbSNP.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:50.180
I'll make one word of caution, the dbSNP is
rather large as you might expect, especially

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:57.530
since in dbSNP release 150 we doubled the
number of RS numbers.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:07.030
Finally, dbVar, which I think is really increasing
in significance.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:13.799
Those structural variants really have big
consequences both in other technical work

00:04:13.799 --> 00:04:18.780
with the human genome as well as well as clinical
reporting consequences.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:25.910
However, some of you may want API access to
this kind of information for the human genome

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:27.840
or other genomes.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:36.090
As many of you are probably aware, we have
an API known to most people as E-Utils and

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:40.540
we've made a command line wrapper for that
API called EDirect.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:47.470
Still, it can sometimes be a little tricky
to use, to make scripts for the commandline

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:54.720
wrapper so one thing we have done is we made
a cookbook on github for scripts using that

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.449
command line interface.

00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:02.500
The amazing thing to me is that we've made
this interactive.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.990
If you have a github account, you can put
in issues for things that you might want to

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:14.669
do with our metadata API or you can just scroll
down, copy some of these scripts and modify

00:05:14.669 --> 00:05:15.669
them.

00:05:15.669 --> 00:05:22.710
For example, if you want not just human but
any taxonomic designation you can grab this

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:30.110
script and simply modify the query sequence
and that would give you whatever taxonomic

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.350
designation you wanted.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:37.190
It does say here for larger sets of data it
may fail with a large number of arguments

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:44.550
and we find empirically that caps out at about
50,000 so searches for more than 50,000 items

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.370
probably won't work so well.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:51.789
You can do a lot of similar things and if
there is something you want to do with API

00:05:51.789 --> 00:05:58.380
metadata crossing NCBI databases, try it and
if you can't figure it out, once again put

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.960
in an issue in the cookbook.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:10.040
The last thing I want to show you today is
how to actually look at the human genome at

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.120
NCBI.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:17.220
And for that what I would do is scroll down
to the view portion of the page and I'm going

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:21.639
to load a resource we have called the genome
data viewer.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:28.319
And this really shows a lot of NCBI resources
at the same time.

00:06:28.319 --> 00:06:34.530
Here I can show a lot of RefSeq transcripts
for particular gene loci.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:44.380
I can show snps in ClinVar, dbSNP and I can
also see RNA-seq coverage and that is pretty

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:45.380
neat.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:53.169
If I want to I can use the tracks menu to
split that out into various tracks.

00:06:53.169 --> 00:07:01.289
One thing I really like about this browser
is I can search for a disease or a gene name

00:07:01.289 --> 00:07:04.120
and it will go ahead and load that.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:13.370
I searched for ApoE and I can see the RefSeq
isoforms for ApoE right here and I can see

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:15.260
related genes here.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:20.550
You can also upload your own data or add tracks
as you wish.

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:27.050
If you want to learn more, we have featured
webinars and there are fact sheets.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:34.300
Many people find these helpful and once again
you can Google for NCBI webinars.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:41.650
You can directly download the latest version
of the human genome sequences Fasta as well

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:50.470
as annotation and associated files right here
at the NCBI human genome resources page.

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:57.220
And with that I'm happy to stop and take any
questions that you may have.

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:05.270
Somebody asked where you can find the common
list of variants reported in all NCBI resources.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:12.120
I would say if you are looking for short variants,
under 50 nucleotides, you will want to look

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:13.440
in dbSNP.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:20.730
And in fact for the dbSNP GRCh38 release,
that's going to be a vcf of all of those variants.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:30.590
Similarly if you are interested in structural
variants, you will want the latest dbVar release.

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:37.140
Both of those resources are available right
here on the human genome resources page.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.979
There are some specific questions.

00:08:39.979 --> 00:08:46.269
I will take a look at them and we may answer
some of them off-line; yes, we will put them

00:08:46.269 --> 00:08:48.730
in the documentation for the webinar.

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:50.230
All right.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:52.350
We are going to go ahead and wrap this up.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:57.850
Thank you very much for your time and I hope
you have an excellent rest of the day.

